>>
endobj
260 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (In October 1923, two women made actuarial history. Dorothy Davies \(1897 - 1977\), aged 26, and Gladys Gregory \(1899 - 1981\), then just 24, became among the first women to qualify as actuaries. That was only four years after the Institute of Actuaries in London opened its exams to female candidates. The milestone was considered so remarkable at the time that it made the headlines of the Daily Mirror.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
261 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (Yet progress remained limited for decades. By October 1925, female members of the Institute consisted of just four Fellows, three Associates and a handful of practitioners. Even by 1951, nearly three decades later, the profession counted only eleven female Fellows.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
