346 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (A profile of Mrs Karuna Bhoojedhur-Obeegadoo, Mauritius' first fully qualified female actuary)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
347 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (It is 4:30pm on a Wednesday afternoon. Over coffee at Paul in So'Flo, the conversation unfolds at its own unhurried pace - the kind that only happens when someone genuinely enjoys where they are and who they are with. The woman across the table has just returned from Ottawa, her schedule packed with board meetings and international travel. Yet she says, without a trace of obligation, that it is always a pleasure to meet the new generation of actuaries.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
348 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (It does not take long to realise something else. She loves to talk. And we are more than happy to listen.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
349 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Mrs Karuna Bhoojedhur-Obeegadoo is Mauritius' first fully qualified female actuary, former Group CEO and Chairperson of SICOM, and former board member of MCB Group Ltd, current board member of MCB Equity Fund Ltd and GuardRisk.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
350 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 93 0 R
/Pg 19 0 R
/A [701 0 R 0]
/K [5]
>>
endobj
351 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Mrs Karuna B Obeegadoo's story begins at Queen Elizabeth College, where she completed her HSC as a laureate. She was drawn to mathematics and physics - and pursued exactly that at A-level, alongside economics. It was a relative who casually suggested actuarial science. She looked it up. LSE offered it. A path was set.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
352 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (There, she also had the opportunity to be in the one of the first ever batches to take up Introduction to Computer Science and learning the ones and zeros.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
353 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (After graduating, she returned to Mauritius and joined SICOM as a clerical officer. The actuarial field in the country was, at the time, barely a handful of people. But she was not going to let that be the end of the story.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (FROM LAUREATE TO LONDON)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (A CEO AT 34)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (After a few years in London, she returned to SICOM. Not long after, an unexpected opportunity came her way - the CEO position. She was thirty-four. A young mother. And a woman.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (She hesitated. Then she said yes.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
358 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (It was 1996. She would go on to lead SICOM, retiring as Group CEO in October 2017 after nearly 30 years with the company. Along the way, she earned recognition that stretched well beyond the island: )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
359 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Jeune Afrique named her among the 38 most influential businesswomen on the African continent)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (- a continental honour that reflected what Mauritius had quietly built in its own backyard. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E <22492054524156454C4C4544204143524F5353205448452049534C414E4420544F2046494E4420535550504F525420544F205052455041524520464F52205448452052455354204F46204D592041435455415249414C204558414D53208420414E594F4E452057484F20434F554C442048454C50204D452046494E44205245534F55524345532C204F522047554944414E43452E22>
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (She began studying for her actuarial exams largely on her own, without structured support or a clear roadmap. The exams were unforgiving. She recalls the first time she failed one - a significant shock. But she reflects on it without bitterness. "Failing forms you," she says. "It shows you that you are not invincible - that you can fall, and that you must get back up." Resilience, she says, is the key chain. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E ("It was very tough," she says. Not once, but twice in the same breath, as if the memory still carries weight. A chance encounter in the corridors of SICOM changed everything: a representative from Swiss Re encouraged her to join their London office. She took the leap.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
364 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E ( And by 28,  she was fully qualified.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
365 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E ("It's not easy," she says, when we ask about balance.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
366 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (The laughter that follows is knowing, not bitter. She returned to work a few weeks after the birth of her second daughter. The responsibility of leading an organisation did not pause - and neither did she. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
367 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (She is quick to acknowledge the support system behind her, particularly the two nannies who have been with her family for over thirty years. But she is equally clear about the sacrifices: her social life stepped back. What she did not sacrifice was presence. She did not miss a single milestone in her daughters' lives. She brought them along on international trips whenever she could - something she believes seeded their love of travel. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
